  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read the dashboard and the rule back to confirm them.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`) points at `kibana_sample_data_logs`. Its time field is `timestamp`. Other main fields:\n   - **Dates:** `@timestamp`, `utc_time`\n   - **Response code:** `response` (text) and `response.keyword` (keyword). The code is stored as a string, not a number.\n   - **Request details:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `agent`, `host`, `referer`, `tags`, `message`, `index`\n   - **Client and location:** `clientip`, `ip`, `ip_range`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.srcdest`, `geo.coordinates`\n   - **Numbers:** `bytes`, `memory`, `phpmemory`, `machine.ram`, and a computed `hour_of_day` field\n   - **Other:** `machine.os` and `event.dataset`\n2. **Dashboard:** I created \"S4-35bd8f7a Web logs\" (id `67dea668-3372-46da-b465-4c55b53a1a07`) with exactly two panels, both using the data view:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of request count over `timestamp`.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart of request count for each `response.keyword` value.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created \"S4-35bd8f7a 5xx errors\" (id `a317ef47-a9dc-49b5-b6be-56b0d6f3acb7`) as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. It matches every response code from 500 to 599 (`response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\"). Because the codes are strings, this range relies on all codes being three digits, which is true for this data. The rule was created enabled, so it didn't need turning on.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\", and the other panel is unchanged. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, and its last run status is `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
